    "product_id": "e5de18a5-9252-49b3-926a-fd1ae889628e",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 021123\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n423 AM MST Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Temperatures should remain in a near to below normal category\n  for the first week of September, resulting in widespread Minor \n  Heat Risk and localized areas of Moderate Heat Risk.\n\n- Isolated showers and storms will be possible over the higher\n  terrain of eastern and southeastern AZ today and Thursday \n\n- Tropical moisture will arrive into the region late Thursday into\n  Friday and persist through much of the weekend, resulting in a\n  chance of heavy rainfall in some locations.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest mid-lvl wv imagery and 500 mb streamline analysis reveals a \ndeep trough of low pressure encompassing much of the West Coast \nwhile a ridge of high pressure extends from the Southern Plains into \nthe southeastern U.S. Our forecast area is situated between both of \nthese features under a prominent fetch of SW flow aloft. Meanwhile,\nTS Marie has formed over the E Pacific, well south of the tip of \nthe Baja Peninsula. Moisture associated with this storm is already\nbeing advected well out ahead of the main circulation into N \nSonora MX as evident in wv imagery. While the storm itself is \nexpected to remain offshore and progress NW into open waters over\nthe coming days, we will begin to see an increase in overall \nmoisture beginning in southern AZ this afternoon and eventually \nencroaching on southcentral AZ by Thursday into Friday. The EPS \nand GEFS mean PWAT values at KPHX show a gradual rise from around \n1.4-1.5\" today to 1.7-1.8\" by Friday with a few members reaching \n2.0\". As moisture advection increases each day, rain chances will \nfollow suit. Latest NBM PoPs are picking up on widely scattered \nshower and storm activity across southeastern AZ this afternoon, \nmainly in the TWC forecast area. On Thursday, a much better push \nof moisture is anticipated which will bring chances for showers \nand storms into southcentral AZ. Currently PoPs are around 30-60% \nlate Thursday into Friday with chances tapering down into Friday \nafternoon. There will likely be a sharp gradient in PoPs from W to\nE on Thursday and Friday as much drier air advects into SE \nCalifornia and SW Arizona. Therefore, rain chances across the \nwestern half of the forecast area will be slim to none initially. \n\nDue to the presence of troughing to our west and a seasonable 500\nmb hght field around 587-590 dam, temperatures will remain within\na degree or two of daily normals through the end of this week. \nLower desert highs will generally range from the upper 90s to \naround 105 degrees each afternoon, following morning lows from the\nupper 70s to middle 80s. The West Coast trough will also induce a\ncompact 700-500 mb hght gradient over southeast CA and western AZ\nbeginning today which will lead to breezy conditions across the \nImperial and Lower Colorado River Valleys. Gusts could reach \nupwards of 25-30 mph in the aforementioned areas and potentially \nas high as 40 mph in far SW Imperial County.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nThe forecast becomes more uncertain heading into this weekend, \nbut the general model consensus is showing the subtropical ridge \nover the southeast U.S. retrograding west into the Southern \nPlains. The western periphery of the ridge will likely nose into \neastern AZ on Saturday. If this occurs, then the subtropical jet \nwill likely shift farther west, setting up better moisture \nadvection over SoCal and western AZ. This scenario is currently \nbeing reflected within the NBM showing a westward progression of \nPoPS from southcentral AZ early Saturday into southwest AZ and \nsoutheast CA late Saturday into Sunday. Due to an overall increase\nin moisture and extensive cloud cover this weekend, it is likely \nwe will see lower instability which could limit convection and \nkeep the threat of heavy rainfall at a minimum in our forecast \nregion with better prospects of heavy rain and flash flooding over\nSoCal and S NV. There is still plenty of time for things to \nchange and we will likely have to wait until we are within the \nhigh-res window for this weekend's forecast to become more clear.\n\nSimilar to rain chances, there is also a high uncertainty in the \ntemperature forecast by this weekend as indicated by large (5-8 \ndegree) spreads within the NBM inner quartile range, especially on \nSaturday. This is likely due to the strength of the ridge and how \nmuch cloud cover will occur over the region. It is likely that \nSaturday will be the coolest day with highs in the lower deserts \nmainly in the mid to upper 90s. However, as ridging aloft causes \nan overall increase in mid lvl hghts/thickness, temperatures may \nwarm to near or even slightly above normal from Sunday into early\nnext week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1122Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected through Wednesday\nafternoon. Winds will tend to follow light familiar diurnal trends\nwith periods of VRB conditions during the overnight and early\nmorning hours and some occasional afternoon gusts in the mid teens\nto around 20 kt. A slower-than-usual transition from east to west\nmay produce a few hours of southerly cross winds at KPHX and KDVT\nlater this morning, with perhaps a window of directions 160V230,\nbefore westerly flow takes over this afternoon. Some hi-res model\nguidance are hinting of TS activity moving within the vicinity of\nthe terminals during the late afternoon to early evening hours,\nbut no mention is included in the TAF at this time as SHRA/TSRA\nchances are too low ~10%. Generally clear skies tonight will give\nway to a FEW low-level clouds during the back half of the period\nbefore skies clear out again tomorrow night.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours.\nWinds will favor a westerly component at KIPL, while south-\nsouthwesterly to southwesterly flow will be common at KBLH. Breezy\nconditions with gusts upwards of 20-25 kt are expected at each\nterminal. Breezy conditions are expected at KBLH during the\nafternoon and early evening hours and at KIPL during the early\nevening hours. Skies will be mostly clear through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier conditions will prevail through Thursday with only isolated \nchances for showers and thunderstorms mostly relegated to the higher \nterrain of southcentral and southeastern AZ. Better chances for \nwetting rains return to the region late this week into this weekend. \nAfternoon minimum humidities will range from 7-15% across Southeast \nCA and between 15-35% for much of Southern AZ through the next few \ndays and will likely increase above 25% areawide this weekend. \nOvernight recoveries will range from poor across the western deserts \nand good to excellent across the eastern districts. Daytime winds \nwill remain elevated out of the south to southwest, with the \nstrongest gusts in Southeast CA and along the Lower Colorado River \nValley peaking between 20-30 mph during the afternoon/early evening.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Berislavich/RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Salerno\n",
